 ## USDC TUNA settlement (implemented locally, not activated)
 
+USDC routing uses `diversity: public-ip`: the guard and edges of each circuit,
+and all providers across sibling circuits, must have distinct public IPs.
+Public ingress and egress may share a provider within a circuit, so two routes
+need at least four distinct IPs. The same operator, beneficiary and hosting ASN
+are allowed; missing ASN metadata does not prevent selection. Equivalent IPv6
+spellings do not count as distinct addresses. This separates endpoints, not
+operators or hosting failure domains, and does not prove separate physical servers.
+Legacy native-NKN diversity policies are unchanged.
+
+The policy label is included in the route policy hash. Upgrade USDC policy
+producers and consumers together and republish routes; old provider-diversity
+records are not silently interpreted as public-IP-only records. Owner-authorized
+registry IDs, current independent qualification, public-address validation,
+two guarded routes, no direct fallback, and spending limits remain required.
+
 `EnclaveConnectivity.authorizeTuna` accepts an owner-signed provider list (one to
 six IDs), expiry, aggregate USDC/GiB cap and a single shared budget. Six allows the
 existing two guarded circuits to use three providers each. `viaTuna` separates
